From 9500384968541b4f6cfa65091d5afeb85d8d5c80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AuraLite <auralite@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 14:57:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] UPKEEP U6: TCP responder sequences its receive path and
 speaks SACK

The TAP responder's TCP peer glued segments onto the DNS stream in
arrival order (rcv_nxt += seglen on anything) and freed the tuple on
a duplicate FIN in LAST_ACK. Any loss or reordering on the wire then
produced acknowledgement numbers that were arithmetic on fiction,
delivered out-of-order bytes as if contiguous, and stranded the
guest's close -- while offering its M6d fast-retransmit machinery
no honest stimulus at all.

Measured first: RFC 9293 3.10.7.4 (acceptability table, trimming,
hold-for-later, challenge ACK, LAST-ACK and TIME-WAIT rules),
RFC 2018 3-4 (8n+2, up to four blocks, first block holds the
triggering segment, no SACK without the peer's SACK-permitted), and
the frozen guest at 0ed0d29 (tcp_m6d.h decodes at most four blocks
with a signed-edge sanity check and only when negotiated; tcp.c
marks its retransmission queue from them and counts duplicate ACKs
per RFC 5681 2, so dup ACKs must stay data-less at a constant
window).

The receiver now validates every segment in modulo-2^32 space
against RCV.NXT and the advertised window, trims duplicates and
both partial overlaps, holds out-of-order data in a bounded queue
(32 runs, bytes bounded by the window; overflow discarded whole
and never SACKed) and delivers only contiguous bytes. The SYN's
SACK-permitted is retained per connection (it used to be parsed and
dropped) and up to four merged SACK blocks ride every ACK while
data is held. FIN is sequence space and is consumed only in order.
A duplicate FIN in LAST_ACK re-sends our FIN|ACK -- the
retransmission timer's job, keyed to the peer's retransmission,
since determinism forbids a clock -- and a bounded FIFO tombstone
table (16 tuples, count-evicted) answers a late duplicate FIN with
the final ACK after the passive close. CLOSING and our-side
TIME_WAIT stay unreachable (no active close exists) and are not
fabricated.

Vectors: 71 checks in test_tcp_sequencing (OOO + gap-fill with
delivery exactly once, partial overlap, two-gap first-block and
drain order, four-block cap, unnegotiated peer, mid-query 2^32
wrap, OOO FIN, duplicate FIN twice + tombstone, beyond-window
discard, right-edge trim, 32-run bound with honest refusal of run
33, FIN-with-data, two-instance byte replay). NC: the same file on
the 0096 tree fails exactly 35 checks, all in the new group. The
unmodified suite stays green on the new code and an old-vs-new A/B
harness matches 16/16 pinned in-order frames byte for byte; the
one deliberate delta (junk after the peer's FIN no longer advances
RCV.NXT) is in the U6 receipts in docs/plans/UPKEEP_PLAN.md.
Gates: make test with both live SeaBIOS ISO lanes and the N1/N2/N3
wire lanes' A/B/D legs green (frozen-guest leg C skips as before),
make test-sanitize green.
